    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Can you explain the difference between high-glycemic index foods and low-glycemic index foods, and specifically why the rate of glucose absorption matters for someone managing insulin response? I'm putting together some notes for a client with Type 2 diabetes.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Glycemic Index (GI) measures how quickly a food raises blood sugar. High-GI foods cause a rapid spike in blood glucose because they are digested very quickly, whereas low-GI foods release sugar slowly into the bloodstream. For someone managing insulin, this difference is crucial because rapid spikes overwhelm the pancreas and insulin's ability to manage the glucose load, leading to dangerous fluctuations. Consuming low-GI options promotes a steadier, more manageable rise in blood sugar levels.",
      "source": "target",
